  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 200 AM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Low stratus and fog mainly from Raleigh south and \neast through mid-morning. Hot weather returns with highs around 90 \ntoday.\n\nSome patchy fog had already developed in the past hour and \nadditional fog/stratus is expected in most areas by 09z. The \nexceptions may be the western and northern Piedmont where the low \nlevel moisture is not as high. \n\nAreas of dense fog will be possible. A dense fog advisory may be \nneeded in some areas from Raleigh south and east to Goldsboro if fog \nis expected to become widespread and less than 1/4 mile.\n\nIt will be hotter today as the upper ridge expands eastward from the \nPlains. Mostly sunny skies by late morning through the afternoon \nwill yield highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...  Peak of the heat will likely be Tuesday through \nThursday with highs in the mid to upper 90s, heat indices of 105+ in \nthe climo favored eastern Piedmont south and east to the coast. Some \ncooling with better chance of PM thunderstorms by Friday. \n\nThe strong upper ridging will gradually build east from OK/KS to the \nTN/NC region by late Thursday. As the surface high shifts offshore, \nthe SW flow will bring high heat and humidity to all the region this \nweek. The guidance is not terribly strong with upper heights as in \nthe early July heat wave in which the H5 heights topped out at \n5990m. This one may peak out in the in the 5950m. or about 40m \nweaker. Regardless, the sun angle is lower, the soil moisture is \nspotty but wetter, it is visually greener, and the NBM continues to \noverheat. It will be an early September heat wave, but more tame \nthan the early July heat wave.\n\nExpect mainly sunny hot days and clear, warm nights Tue-Thursday \nnight. Lows in the 70s. Highs in the mid to upper 90s. Heat indices \nof 105-109 from Raleigh south and eastward Wednesday and Thursday \n(when this heat wave peaks for us). \n\nBy Friday, a disturbance in the NW flow should beat down the upper \nridge from the Great Lakes into the Mid-Atlantic states late in the \nday through Saturday. Highs Friday (lower to mid 90s) with a chance \nof PM thunderstorms.  \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3... Labor Day weekend will be cooler with a lingering \nchance of showers/storms south and east Saturday. Sunday and Labor \nDay should be mostly dry with highs in the 80s. \n\nThe mid/upper heights fall Friday and Saturday, with a surface front \nlikely to push south and east to near the Coast Saturday. The better \nchances of storms should push to the south and east of the Triangle \nSaturday. Sunday and Labor Day have been trending drier and cooler. \nExpect highs in the 80s. Lows in the 60s to near 70 SE.",
